WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.680
In the age of YouTube and fanatic fandoms,&nbsp;
something either has to be a Masterpiece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.480
or an atrocity to garner more views and&nbsp;
attention. But Ahsoka is really neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.880
of those things and at least to me falls&nbsp;
right into the middle and is just decent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.160
I’m not going to sit here and complain&nbsp;
about how females have taken over Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.800
Wars or whatever that complaint is and just&nbsp;
care about the pure quality of the show. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.840
are definite strengths Ahsoka has and there&nbsp;
are also some big issues that hold it back.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.360
First, going into what I think is the show’s major&nbsp;
flaw that really hindered me from enjoying it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.600
the fact that there are no stakes or tension.&nbsp;
That’s primarily in reference to an action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.000
perspective, but before going into that, the&nbsp;
show’s inherent premise is already spoiled from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.920
the get-go so you always know exactly where the&nbsp;
story is going, thus destroying any suspense as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.560
to what happens. Because throughout, the show&nbsp;
treats the question and threat as to if Thrawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.720
is returning as this big point of mystery and&nbsp;
conflict. However, through marketing and them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.040
publicly casting Thrawn, we know right away that&nbsp;
he will return. It becomes this inevitability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.640
instead of a question as to if it will happen.&nbsp;
Nothing the characters do have stakes because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.160
already know the outcome beforehand. This isn’t&nbsp;
necessarily a fault to the writing and instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.920
the inherent nature of the show and how it was&nbsp;
packaged. Although, I think the story does a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.120
job at combatting this by having our protagonists&nbsp;
overcome obstacles in more creative ways after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.400
a simple solution is snuffed out. Like in the&nbsp;
beginning when the information from the puzzle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.080
was stolen and negotiating with a space whale for&nbsp;
safe passage to a new galaxy. So even though we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.440
know the outcome, the process of getting there is&nbsp;
still relatively interesting and not very obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.360
It holds just enough of a hook within the audience&nbsp;
to keep your attention throughout the series. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.040
going back to the tension and stakes for action&nbsp;
scenes, this is an aspect about the show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.720
really drove me crazy. In every single lightsaber&nbsp;
fight scene besides the final fight with Morgan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.520
where finally someone actually dies, the&nbsp;
heroes and villains walk away unscathed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.160
it’s as if were just watching skirmishes with no&nbsp;
consequences. It becomes extremely contrived for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.680
characters to survive these combat encounters,&nbsp;
but the creators want to artificially inject more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.680
action into the series. And through each action&nbsp;
scene, they expose the plot armor these characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.160
are wearing more and more. Like the most egregious&nbsp;
example is Sabine. We have the infamous opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.560
where she gets skewered by a lightsaber and&nbsp;
is fine, but the writers hilariously do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.080
again essentially by having Shin simply leave&nbsp;
before killing Sabine in episode 4. She disarms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.880
her and knocks her onto the ground, but Sabine&nbsp;
shoots her once, Shin drops her lightsaber, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.560
then peaces out with a smoke bomb. Like she could&nbsp;
easily just kill her here since she lost track of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.600
her and can force grab her lightsaber, but heyyy&nbsp;
we need Sabine for later. Then when she shows up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.680
to stop Baylan and grabs the artifact, Baylan can&nbsp;
simply force pull it out of her hand and kill her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.960
but he instead opts to make a deal to artificially&nbsp;
keep her alive. Every episode is full of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:55.640
glaring examples of plot armor and contrivances&nbsp;
to the point that it invalidates all danger. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.040
know that every single lightsaber fight ends&nbsp;
with no casualties and it’s insane that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.440
have about 3 of them and nothing major happens.&nbsp;
Without any tension or stakes it makes the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:11.520
extremely dull and generally what saves action if&nbsp;
that is the case is great choreography. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.000
I wouldn’t say a single lightsaber fight in the&nbsp;
show was remarkable and all came off as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.480
bland. And the big issue is that we have so many&nbsp;
examples of great lightsaber fights to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.200
that if you ever draw a comparison from those to&nbsp;
Ahsoka they utterly blow Ahsoka out of the water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.000
They aren’t dynamic, the lightsaber choreography&nbsp;
is generally pretty generic with rare spicy moves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.120
the choreography is at times slow and clunky&nbsp;
as if they didn’t have enough rehearsal time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.160
and even the locations in which the fight scenes&nbsp;
takes place does nothing for the action. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:48.080
another aspect that I think hurts these lightsaber&nbsp;
fights is the constant usage of parallel editing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.080
Now parallel editing is when you cut back and&nbsp;
forth between two separate scenes happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.640
at the same time within the story. Generally,&nbsp;
this is fine, but the way they utilize parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.640
editing in Ahsoka is at times really annoying.&nbsp;
Mainly because they spend all this time building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.360
up to a lightsaber showdown and then the&nbsp;
second they start fighting they cut away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:11.040
to another scene. It robs you of that excitement&nbsp;
you slowly build up leading to this major moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.880
just for it to instantly dissipate. At times&nbsp;
it feels like a copout because their budget may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:20.720
not be big enough to show us an uninterrupted&nbsp;
lightsaber duel, and instead just small chunks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.520
here and there. I want to see the whole story of&nbsp;
the fight and the ebb and flow of who is winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.520
instead of jarringly being thrown in and out of&nbsp;
it. Then every other type of action scene is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.680
plagued with the same issues of constant usages&nbsp;
of contrivances and plot armor. From spaceship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.360
combat being completely nonsensical in which&nbsp;
enemy fighters can endlessly shoot at Ahsoka’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.840
ship and constantly miss and do nothing.&nbsp;
Or if they do damage it like in the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:50.600
Huyang is able to repair it in like 30 minutes&nbsp;
somehow and there’s just no consequences. Or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.227
normal fights with stormtroopers in which they&nbsp;
are never shooting the protagonists when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:58.880
are in clear sight. The biggest example was when&nbsp;
they were all surrounded at the end of episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.600
7 and when Ahsoka shows up they literally all&nbsp;
just start running away instead of overwhelming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.320
her with blasters. Or literally just becoming&nbsp;
immortal and rushing an open field with a star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.160
destroyer reigning hell down on them and none&nbsp;
of them sustaining any casualties. Like I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.760
Obi-wan and The Book of Boba Fett were bad,&nbsp;
but holy shit these examples are on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:24.360
level and if not even worse at times. I know Dave&nbsp;
Filoni is a more competent writer than Jon Favreau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.800
which I’ll get into in a second, but he still&nbsp;
suffers from regularly using contrivances to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.280
make his story work. The infuriating thing about&nbsp;
Ahsoka is that if these major issues were fixed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:39.040
the show would be significantly better. But it’s&nbsp;
just plagued by that cheap Disney+ show feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.920
where all the action feels rushed, botched&nbsp;
and plagued with apparent writing issues.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.040
Moving onto another aspect, let’s go into the&nbsp;
characters. Ironically, and this is rarely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.320
case, I loved all the antagonists and none of&nbsp;
the protagonists. I didn’t hate them mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.880
think they are pretty generic with not a lot&nbsp;
interesting happening under the hood. Ahsoka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.560
was devoid of personality and was just this&nbsp;
simple zen-like figure. So much to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:15.040
that she kind of gave me pretentious vibes as&nbsp;
to this is how Jedi should act. When in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.160
all the most interesting Jedi characters had very&nbsp;
colorful personalities and had certain quirks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:26.000
made them so loveable. Like Obi-Wan’s civil nature&nbsp;
and Anakin’s wreckless albeit effective nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.080
Ahsoka doesn’t really have anything going for her&nbsp;
and she mostly comes across as a dull figure. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.120
doesn’t have a character arc in her own show&nbsp;
and even the quote-on-quote best part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.760
series about her flashback doesn’t really add&nbsp;
much to her character. I liked this sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.280
and enjoyed it, but if you really take a look&nbsp;
at it within the context of the entire season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.560
you could effectively remove it and nothing&nbsp;
changes unfortunately. It’s cool to see her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.920
confront Anakin again and their discussion was&nbsp;
infinitely more intriguing than anything with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.680
Sabine, so much to the point that I’d rather we’d&nbsp;
just have gotten a live-action Clone Wars show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.280
but Ahsoka really doesn’t learn any revelations&nbsp;
along with this introducing more mechanics within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:07.520
the universe that are confusing. Like if a Jedi&nbsp;
is on the brink of death, do they always go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.000
this mind palace area where they fight for their&nbsp;
life that conveniently slows down their heart rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.200
for an hour as they await to be saved? I like&nbsp;
this concept in theory, but it’s implementation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.680
is undeniably messy and kind of confusing. Not&nbsp;
to mention that Hera randomly brings her force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:27.640
sensitive son to a dangerous combat mission&nbsp;
just so that he can hear Ahsoka and Anakin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:32.920
lightsaber duel. Which essentially brings up the&nbsp;
point that for every good aspect in the show it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:37.960
immediately counterbalanced by something bad. And&nbsp;
my main assumption is that Star Wars fans are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.120
blind to these faults or are much more accepting&nbsp;
and forgiving of them. I’ve seen Star Wars fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.760
say this scene gave them full body chills and I&nbsp;
just don’t understand that level of attachment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.640
this franchise anymore given Disney’s handling&nbsp;
of the IP. Like we were all like that for The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.720
Force Awakens, but in this day and age after so&nbsp;
much heinous shit they’ve done to the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:03.000
I don’t have that level of blind hype for Star&nbsp;
Wars and I’m more critical now. Then for Sabine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.400
I see a lot of hate for her character in that she&nbsp;
shouldn’t be force sensitive, but I don’t care if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:12.600
she has two X chromosomes. Especially since The&nbsp;
Last Jedi establishes the notion that anyone can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.200
be force sensitive and has to be unlocked with&nbsp;
intense training. And some people are obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.440
more powerful than others. I don’t know if people&nbsp;
are just deleting this out of their personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.720
headcanon, and at this point in the Star Wars&nbsp;
universe, there have been so many cooks in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.600
kitchen that there is so much conflicting points&nbsp;
within the lore. So since I’m defending her here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.120
you may assume I like the character, and that&nbsp;
would be wrong because I don’t. I found her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.160
be largely annoying given her dominant selfish and&nbsp;
rebellious nature. Which is fine from a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.000
standpoint, but the writers utterly screw her&nbsp;
over in episode 4 by her choosing to condemn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.960
the entire galaxy. She chooses to bring back&nbsp;
Thrawn which will most likely lead to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.800
war just so that she can see her friend again.&nbsp;
This event immediately disconnects you from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.840
character and I personally grew so much hatred&nbsp;
for her because of this. Dave Filoni thinks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.560
this gives the character more depth because she’s&nbsp;
missing that connection of family within her life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.040
but at the entire expense of the galaxy? Really?&nbsp;
It just doesn’t seem justifiable at all and I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:14.400
really surprised she isn’t even condemned for&nbsp;
doing this later on. Like she literally faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:19.400
no consequences, or at least so far. There’s also&nbsp;
some other characters that I won’t touch on for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:24.240
the sake of time like Hera who I thought was fine&nbsp;
and Huyang. Out of all of them though, I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.640
enjoyed Huyang the most which kind of feels&nbsp;
sad for the state of the main characters. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.600
the antagonists, the clear standout is obviously&nbsp;
Thrawn. Like holy shit, I knew he was a favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.880
but when he shows up the show goes from mediocre&nbsp;
to great. His screen presence is immediately felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:44.640
and surges with so much menacing energy that&nbsp;
you genuinely feel fear just by him staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.560
at you. Which is also a testament to the&nbsp;
brilliant design of the character. The cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.680
calculating, and meticulous nature of the&nbsp;
character is so fresh in comparison to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:59.280
empire villains who generally feel more cartoonish&nbsp;
and mustache twirly. He’s never underestimating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.680
his threat and is actually strategizing in real&nbsp;
time against his opponents. Star Wars protagonists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:08.920
were always able to exploit the fact that&nbsp;
they were underestimated by their adversaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:13.800
but here, it seems like Thrawn is actually trying&nbsp;
his best to win. Although the story is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.560
pretty contrived in that he could simply order a&nbsp;
tie fighter strike on the hermit crab convoy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:24.240
fucking decimate them, but heyyy, what do I know&nbsp;
about military strategy, I just have a useless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.920
bachelors degree in film studies. At the very&nbsp;
least though he actually wins and it makes season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:34.000
2 or I guess a movie maybe, actually exciting&nbsp;
because there’s a lot of potential for what can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.880
done with the character and his story. I’d assume&nbsp;
they’d overcome and kill him in the end, but how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.800
much damage he does and who he kills before then&nbsp;
is the main question. Then there are also some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.960
other Sith characters that are generally pretty&nbsp;
intriguing; especially Baylan. The character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:52.600
has a lot of class and he’s actually honorable&nbsp;
and is pretty witty. He has a lot of depth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.760
history and like Thrawn, he has a great screen&nbsp;
presence. It’s just too bad that the main story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.440
for his character is primarily being reserved for&nbsp;
the next season and it’s also unfortunate that Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.840
Stevenson passed away. Hopefully, they recast him&nbsp;
and don’t leave him on that literal cliffhanger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:12.520
in perpetual limbo. Then finally we have Shin who&nbsp;
I also think has an infectious screen presence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:17.040
She’s so expressive with her eyes and so much hate&nbsp;
pours through them to the point that she’s cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:21.200
into your soul. She does a lot with so little&nbsp;
which I think is a testament to the how great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.000
of an actor she is. Along with her character&nbsp;
having such an off-kilter design that it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.240
her general figure kind of disturbing and&nbsp;
terrifying. It’s just a bit unfortunate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.120
she didn’t have a lot to do within the story&nbsp;
and she was kind of screwed over by the many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:39.160
contrivances of the writing, but hopefully, she&nbsp;
has a bigger role in season 2 with more of an arc.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:42.680
Going into some more positives for the&nbsp;
show, I think the overall productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:48.200
and pace was much better than Jon Favaeru’s in The&nbsp;
Mandalorian. Because if this was written by him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:53.040
the season would have ended with the reveal of&nbsp;
Thrawn in episode 6 and in that time he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.720
have injected a bunch of bullshit sidequests&nbsp;
to pad out the length of the season. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:03.280
Dave Filoni delivers a real and coherent pace to&nbsp;
the point that it’s funnily a positive aspect now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.920
Like this should be something that’s a given.&nbsp;
I’ve seen some people still complain that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:11.160
show is too slow, but I think a lot of the down&nbsp;
moments are still filled with productive usage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.240
of character development. Albeit, quite a&nbsp;
bit of it isn’t engaging enough to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:19.920
be that entertaining. And I understand the&nbsp;
critique that nothing happens in the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:24.520
which is flat-out wrong, but people probably think&nbsp;
that because the show was essentially pitched as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:29.000
Thrawn returning and that doesn’t happen till the&nbsp;
end of the season. Which again is a problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:34.000
how they sold the show. Dave Filoni at times also&nbsp;
delivers some genuinely great moments within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.840
story like zombie stormtroopers. I think that&nbsp;
was easily the best setpiece and was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:42.960
a moment where I felt an ounce of danger for&nbsp;
our heroes because the enemy was threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:47.680
for once. Although they’re literally wielding&nbsp;
lightsabers and can dismember them, but whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.240
that’s more of a nitpick instead of a major&nbsp;
issue. Also another aspect about his writing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:57.480
his directing that I think he nails is the tone.&nbsp;
It really reminds me of old-school Star Wars and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:02.560
what it was even inspired by which was Akira&nbsp;
Kurosawa. George Lucas was heavily influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:07.240
by his Samurai films and essentially took the&nbsp;
samurai, gave them laser swords and the force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:11.760
and shipped them off to a galaxy far far away&nbsp;
and called them Jedi. Dave Filoni harnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.560
this original inspiration and applies it to&nbsp;
great effect in Ahsoka. Like the build-up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.280
every fight being slow and methodical as&nbsp;
the music slowly builds your excitement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:25.800
An example I still love is the first fight scene&nbsp;
where Ahsoka is ambushed by some droids who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:31.200
also hunting for the map. Their introduction is&nbsp;
intimidating and mysterious and just hammers home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.600
that samurai tone so well. Just don’t think&nbsp;
about how they don’t instantly activate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:40.560
self-destruct and conveniently wait for Ahsoka&nbsp;
to clear the area. Again for every good aspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.240
there has to be something that weighs it&nbsp;
down. Then since I briefly brought it up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.600
I think the music was great throughout the&nbsp;
series. It’s very prominent in Ahsoka and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:53.760
really emphasizes and elevates each scene. Along&nbsp;
with it expertly punctuating select important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:59.000
moments. I really love the end credit theme and&nbsp;
pretty much every show besides maybe Obi-Wan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.200
has had outstanding music. The Mandalorian&nbsp;
though is still top tier and that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:08.120
because Ludwig Gorranson is basically the next&nbsp;
Hans Zimmer. So yeah, from an overall outlook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:13.080
I think Ahsoka is kind of mediocre, and just by&nbsp;
having this opinion, I assume this video won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:17.760
do well since I’m not playing to any of the&nbsp;
two extreme sides of love or hate. There’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:22.480
lot more nuance surrounding Ahsoka in terms of&nbsp;
strengths and flaws and to me, the best way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:27.480
fix some of those issues is if Dave Filoni wasn’t&nbsp;
the sole writer for the show. Through Andor, we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.800
gotten a taste of what great Star Wars writing&nbsp;
could look like instead of the shallow adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:44.720
we got. Thank you for watching and let me know&nbsp;
in the comments what you thought about Ahsoka.

